ARTICLES = [
 {
  "slug": "tpe-vs-pvc-vs-rubber-car-mats-importer-guide",
  "title": "TPE vs PVC vs Rubber Car Mats: What Importers Must Know",
  "desc": "TPE, PVC or rubber car mats: how odour, cold flexibility, weight and moulding accuracy change your landed cost, your returns rate and your price point.",
  "kind": "Guide",
  "date": "2026-09-20",
  "body": "<p>Floor mats look like a simple commodity line. In a container order they are not, because the material you choose sets your retail price point, the weight you pay to ship, and the kind of complaint your customer service team will handle for the next two years. This guide compares the three families that dominate wholesale mat programmes - TPE, flexible PVC and rubber - against the criteria that actually move money for an importer.</p>\n\n<h2>What each material really is</h2>\n<p><strong>TPE</strong> is a thermoplastic elastomer: a rubber-like material that can be melted and re-melted. Mats are injection moulded or thermoformed from it, and because the surface does not have to be softened with a liquid plasticiser, TPE mat grades are normally formulated without one. That is where the low-odour story comes from.</p>\n<p><strong>PVC</strong> in mat form is soft, flexible PVC. The flexibility comes from plasticiser added at a high loading, and that formulation controls smell, colour and cold behaviour. It is the cheapest of the three by a wide margin and the easiest to colour-match.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubber</strong> here means vulcanised or EPDM-based sheet, die-cut or moulded. It is dense, heavy, and cannot be re-melted. A rubber mat feels substantial in the hand, which is exactly why it survives in some markets.</p>\n\n<h2>Six differences that decide the order</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Odour and plasticiser.</strong> A plasticiser-heavy PVC mat in a closed cabin on a hot day is the most common odour complaint in this category. TPE grades are typically plasticiser-free, so that complaint largely disappears.</li>\n<li><strong>Cold flexibility.</strong> Plasticised PVC stiffens as temperature falls and can turn brittle. TPE stays pliable at the same temperatures, which matters if you sell into a market with a real winter. Rubber stays flexible too, but it is stiff to begin with.</li>\n<li><strong>Recyclability.</strong> TPE scrap can be re-ground and re-used inside the factory. PVC can also be re-processed, but the plasticiser content makes clean closed-loop reuse harder. Vulcanised rubber is effectively a one-way material.</li>\n<li><strong>Halogen content.</strong> PVC contains chlorine as part of its chemistry; TPE and rubber compounds used for mats are generally formulated without halogens. If halogen content is a question in your market, ask the factory for the material declaration that applies there instead of assuming.</li>\n<li><strong>Weight and shipping.</strong> At equal thickness, a TPE liner is the lightest of the three per square metre, PVC sits in the middle, and rubber is the heaviest by a clear margin.</li>\n<li><strong>Moulding accuracy.</strong> TPE and thermoformed PVC can both be moulded to a scanned footwell, but the practical difference is tooling intent: a full-coverage liner is built as a tray against a vehicle-specific tool, while a cheaper mat is often cut from sheet and sold as a semi-universal fit.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Weight, volume and the shipping invoice</h2>\n<p>Freight is billed on kilograms and cubic metres, and the two do not always agree. A heavy rubber mat line can look cheap per piece at FOB and lose its advantage by the time it lands in your warehouse, because every set carries dead weight across an ocean. A light TPE liner does the opposite: the per-set price is higher, but you move more sets per container and pay less per unit of freight.</p>\n<p>Before you commit to a material, ask for the master carton dimensions, the gross weight per carton and the number of sets per carton. That single table lets you compare landed cost per set rather than FOB price per set, and it is the number that decides whether the programme makes money. It also sets your e-commerce shipping band, since a bulky mat pushes a parcel into the next weight tier.</p>\n\n<h2>Moulding accuracy decides your returns rate</h2>\n<p>Most \"it does not fit\" returns are not a material problem, they are a tooling problem. A moulded tray that follows the footwell, with raised side walls and the retention points of the vehicle, sits where it is supposed to sit. A sheet mat that has been trimmed to a generic outline leaves gaps along the sill, slides forward under the pedals, and comes back. Look at the tool reference in the spec sheet on a product page - for example the Toyota Corolla liner built on tool W-68 - because a named tool tells you the part was built against one specific pattern rather than bought in. The full range of moulded tray products sits in <a href=\"/floor-liners/\">3D TPE floor liners</a>, while flatter mats are listed separately under <a href=\"/all-weather-mats/\">all-weather floor mats</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Where PVC is still the right choice</h2>\n<p>Being honest about this saves arguments later. PVC wins when the market buys on price and nothing else, when you need several colours on a showroom floor at a low entry cost, when the vehicle is old and the buyer wants a flat mat rather than a tray, and when fitment tolerance genuinely does not matter because the end user trims it themselves. If your returns are handled locally at low cost and your end users expect a heavy, thick-feeling mat, a well-formulated PVC product can still work.</p>\n<p>What loses money is buying PVC for a cold market and then absorbing winter complaints, or buying it for a premium listing and competing against a moulded liner on appearance.</p>\n\n<h2>Where rubber still earns its freight</h2>\n<p>Rubber holds on in commercial applications: vans, taxis, site vehicles and fleets where the mat is treated as a consumable and the buyer wants weight and grip. It is also the material some wholesale buyers simply expect, and fighting that expectation costs more than it saves. The trade-off is honest: more kilos per set, a material that cannot be re-melted, and a limited colour range.</p>\n\n<h2>What the fit-and-finish trade-off looks like</h2>\n<p>TPE's weakness is presentation, not performance. A thin, flexible tray can read as less substantial than a thick rubber mat on a shelf, and some end users equate weight with quality. If that is your market, the answer is usually a thicker liner or a detachable two-layer construction rather than a change of material family - for example the <a href=\"/products/floor-liners-4/\">10 mm thickened TPE floor liner</a> listing, or the <a href=\"/double-layer-mats/\">double-layer mats</a> range where the top layer lifts out for cleaning.</p>\n\n<h2>Complaints to expect, by material</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PVC:</strong> cabin odour, edge curl after shipping while rolled, stiffness and cracking in winter markets.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubber:</strong> freight cost disputes, smell from some compounds, and no resale value for scrap.</li>\n<li><strong>TPE:</strong> fewer odour and cold-weather returns, but occasional \"feels light\" feedback and complaints that trace back to tooling rather than material.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>What to ask your supplier in writing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Material family, the specific grade name, and thickness in millimetres.</li>\n<li>Whether a plasticiser is used, and how plasticiser migration is controlled if your end market fits light trim.</li>\n<li>The tool reference behind each part number, and whether it is an existing mould or a new one.</li>\n<li>Laboratory reports for the market you sell into - odour and emission, flammability, heavy metals, phthalates - with the measured parameters and the method written on the document itself. Do not accept a file with no scope page; ask what was actually measured.</li>\n<li>Carton dimensions, gross weight, sets per carton and CBM per set.</li>\n<li>Colour reference and logo artwork, with the approval step written into the order.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Next step: compare on landed cost, not FOB</h2>\n<p>Take two or three vehicles you actually sell - a left-hand-drive pickup such as the <a href=\"/shop-by-vehicle/toyota/hilux-revo-2015-2025/\">Toyota Hilux Revo 2015-2025</a> is a good test case because tray liners live or die on fit - and ask for the same quantity in TPE, PVC and rubber with carton and weight data. If you want that comparison run against your own vehicle list, <a href=\"/#finder\">send the make, model and year</a> and we will check the tooling we hold and quote against it.</p>"
 },
 {
  "slug": "how-to-specify-custom-fit-floor-liners",
  "title": "How to Specify Custom-Fit Floor Liners: Importer Checklist",
  "desc": "A step-by-step specification checklist for custom-fit floor liner orders: year and generation, drive side, positions, fixing points, samples and PO wording.",
  "kind": "Guide",
  "date": "2026-09-20",
  "body": "<p>A custom-fit order fails in one of two ways: the factory ships something that does not match the car, or it ships something that matches the car but not the product you thought you were buying. Both are avoidable at the specification stage, and both are expensive once the container has sailed. This is the checklist we use with importers, distributors and accessory wholesalers before a floor liner programme is tooled.</p>\n\n<h2>Define what \"custom fit\" means for this order</h2>\n<p>Custom fit is not one product. A moulded tray is built against a pattern taken from the specific vehicle and has raised side walls that follow the footwell. A trimmed mat is cut from sheet to an outline that is close to the footwell but not derived from it. Both are sold as custom fit in the trade, and they land at different retail prices. Decide which one the order is for before anything else, then write it into the specification.</p>\n<p>If you need full coverage with high walls, you are buying from the <a href=\"/floor-liners/\">3D TPE floor liners</a> range. If you need boot protection rather than footwell protection, the equivalent category is <a href=\"/trunk-mats/\">trunk mats and cargo liners</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Confirm year, generation and body type</h2>\n<p>A model name is not a fitment. Two cars with the same badge can have different floor pans after a facelift, and the same model often ships in more than one body style. Give the factory all of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Make and model, written the way the local market writes it.</li>\n<li>Model year or year range, and the generation code where one exists.</li>\n<li>Body type - sedan, hatch, SUV, pickup, and for pickups the cab configuration.</li>\n<li>Market variant, if the vehicle is built differently for your region.</li>\n<li>Any trim level that changes the interior, such as a third row or a centre console layout.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"/shop-by-vehicle/honda/cr-v/\">Honda CR-V fitment page</a> shows the level of detail worth aiming for: vehicle, drive side, coverage and the tool behind the part, all on one page you can check before you order.</p>\n\n<h2>Settle left-hand drive versus right-hand drive</h2>\n<p>Mirror-image cabins need mirror-image tooling, and a factory that holds left-hand-drive tools cannot produce a right-hand-drive part without a new mould. Drive side is therefore one of the first questions, not a detail. Ask for it in writing on the quotation, so a later reorder cannot drift. Products in the range state their drive side explicitly - the <a href=\"/products/toyota-hilux-vigo-dual-cab-floor-liners/\">Hilux Vigo dual cab liner</a> is listed as left-hand-drive tooling with the cab configuration named.</p>\n\n<h2>List every position you are covering</h2>\n<p>Positions drive piece count, cost and carton size. Write them out rather than saying \"full set\":</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Front row, driver and passenger, and whether a one-piece front liner is acceptable.</li>\n<li>Second row, and whether the transmission tunnel is covered.</li>\n<li>Third row, if the vehicle has one.</li>\n<li>Trunk or cargo area, including whether the load floor is flat or has a sub-floor well.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is the point where a three-piece and a five-piece set stop being interchangeable. Some listings, such as the <a href=\"/products/floor-liners-6/\">custom printed 5-piece liner</a>, are built around a fixed piece count, so confirm the count matches your retail listing before tooling.</p>\n\n<h2>Fixing points and retention</h2>\n<p>Modern footwells retain mats through posts, clips or hooks in the floor. Decide whether you need the liner moulded to accept those points, whether you will supply aftermarket clips, and who pays for the fixing hardware if it is included. A liner that ignores the retention points slides forward, which is both a returns problem and a safety conversation you do not want.</p>\n\n<h2>Raised edge height and coverage</h2>\n<p>Edge height is what separates a deep tray from a light all-season mat, and it changes both the clean-up story and the carton bulk. Ask for the edge height in millimetres and a photo of the tray section on the approval sample. Also agree whether the liner covers the sill trim and the lower console sides, or stops at the carpet edge.</p>\n\n<h2>Colour, thickness and branding</h2>\n<p>Decide colour against the interior you are selling into, and decide thickness in millimetres rather than by feel. Branding is usually the last item to be settled and the first to cause a delay, so treat it as its own approval step: logo file in a vector format, position, size, and whether it is embossed into the mould or printed. Embossed branding is fixed in the tool and cannot be changed later without a new one; printed branding stays flexible. The <a href=\"/products/toyota-crown-floor-liners/\">Toyota Crown liner</a> listing is one example where logo printing for buyer labels is offered, so treat printed branding as a separate approval line on the order.</p>\n\n<h2>Approve a sample before the bulk run</h2>\n<p>Ask for a production-representative sample set, not a photo, and test it in the actual vehicle: retention points, pedal clearance, edge contact along the sill, and whether the tray stays put when someone gets in. Approve in writing with the sample reference and date recorded, and keep one sealed unit as the reference against which bulk is checked.</p>\n\n<h2>What the purchase order must state</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vehicle, model year or year range, generation and body type, for every SKU on the order.</li>\n<li>Drive side, position by position, and the piece count per set.</li>\n<li>Material, grade where agreed, thickness in millimetres, and colour reference.</li>\n<li>The tool reference or master sample number the bulk must be produced against.</li>\n<li>Logo artwork file, placement and method, with the approved artwork revision number.</li>\n<li>Fixing hardware or clip requirement, and whether it is included in the set.</li>\n<li>Carton configuration, sets per carton, carton marking and barcode requirement.</li>\n<li>Approved sample reference and approval date, stated as the quality baseline.</li>\n<li>Quantity per variant, allowing a stated tolerance on the total, and the inspection arrangement before shipment.</li>\n<li>Shipping terms, documents required and the shipping marks to be printed on the cartons.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Then send the list</h2>\n<p>The fastest way to close all of the above is to send one table: make, model, year, drive side, positions and quantity per vehicle. <a href=\"/#finder\">Tell us the make, model and year for each vehicle</a> and we will check our tooling against it, flag anything that needs a new mould, and confirm fitment before a price is issued.</p>"
 },
 {
  "slug": "oem-car-mat-sourcing-vehicle-scan-to-container",
  "title": "OEM Car Mat Sourcing: From Vehicle Scan to Container",
  "desc": "How an OEM car mat programme runs: tooling choice, first-article samples, QC checks, carton marking, container loading, compression and customs paperwork.",
  "kind": "Guide",
  "date": "2026-09-20",
  "body": "<p>An OEM mat programme is a sequence of decisions, not a single purchase. Each stage produces something you can check, and each one is cheaper to fix than the stage after it. This is the shape of the process from the moment a vehicle is scanned to the moment a container is closed, written for importers and distributors running their own brand.</p>\n\n<h2>Start with the tooling decision</h2>\n<p>Everything downstream depends on whether the pattern already exists. If we hold a mould for your vehicle, the project starts at sampling: you see a part quickly and the cost sits in the goods. If the vehicle is not in the tool library, a new mould has to be built from a scan first, which adds a development stage, a cost line and an approval loop in front of production. Both routes can be right - the mistake is ordering a product that needs a new tool without knowing it.</p>\n<p>Ranges in this catalogue are built from named tools, and the tool reference is quoted on the product page so you can see whether the part is a genuine vehicle-specific mould. For example, the <a href=\"/products/toyota-hilux-revo-2015-2025-floor-liners/\">Hilux Revo 2015-2025 liner</a> is produced from a single tool for a defined year band, and the <a href=\"/shop-by-vehicle/toyota/hilux-revo-2015-2025/\">vehicle page for the same model</a> gathers the fitment information on one screen.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 1: vehicle scan and pattern</h2>\n<p>A pattern starts with the actual car. The footwell is scanned or measured directly, then converted into a solid model that accounts for draft angles, wall thickness, the ridge line along the sill, the retention points and the amount the material will shrink as it cools. If you are supplying the vehicle rather than borrowing a local one, say so at the start - access to the car is the single most common reason a new-mould project sits still. Cargo liners follow the same route but start from the load floor, which is why the <a href=\"/trunk-mats/\">trunk mats and cargo liners</a> range is tooled separately from footwell parts.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 2: first-article samples</h2>\n<p>You want a first-article set, not a photograph. The useful test is physical: drop it into the vehicle, clip it to the retention points, and drive. Then check the things photographs hide - edge contact along the sill, clearance at the pedals, whether the tray lifts when a passenger gets in, and how the material behaves cold. Feedback at this stage is a note and a revision. Feedback after the mould is cut is a new tool.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 3: QC checkpoints that catch real problems</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Part dimensions and silhouette checked against the master sample, position by position.</li>\n<li>Retention point positions and clip engagement.</li>\n<li>Moulded edge height and wall thickness read at agreed points.</li>\n<li>Surface condition - flow marks, short shots, flash, weld lines, warping.</li>\n<li>Colour against the approved reference swatch under the same light.</li>\n<li>Logo placement against the approved artwork revision.</li>\n<li>Odour checked on a freshly unpacked carton, not on a sample that has been airing on a desk.</li>\n<li>Backing or anti-slip layer integrity, and clip hardware contents.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Agree the checklist and the sampling plan with the factory before production starts, and agree who signs off. A quality agreement that only lives in an email thread is worth nothing.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 4: packing and carton marking</h2>\n<p>Mats ship as sets, so the packing decision is whether the set is bagged together or bagged per piece, and whether the parts are stacked by position to make retail assembly quick. Master carton strength matters more than it looks: liners are bulky and light, so cartons get stacked high and crushed at the corners if the board grade is chosen for cost alone.</p>\n<p>Carton marking should carry the SKU, vehicle description, set count per carton, quantity, gross weight, carton dimensions, your shipping marks and a barcode if your warehouse scans. For a range like <a href=\"/double-layer-mats/\">double-layer mats</a>, where the set contains two separable layers, add a line confirming that the top and base layers of the same size are packed together - mixed cartons are a warehouse problem you will pay for twice.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 5: container loading and compression</h2>\n<p>Liners nest, which is the reason the freight maths works in your favour. Sets are compressed into stacks with strapping before loading, so the container is filled by volume rather than by air. Two loading styles are worth comparing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Floor loaded.</strong> Compression-packed stacks go straight on the container floor, giving the highest fill for a single-SKU order. You need a realistic loading plan so the door end is not packed with loose cartons.</li>\n<li><strong>Palletised.</strong> Faster to receive and easier to handle in your warehouse, at the cost of some container fill.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ask for a load plan with the quote, and check whether the mix of cartons you ordered actually fills the container you booked. A firm load plan also tells you whether the quote was built on volume or on weight, which decides which way the price moves when you add SKUs. The same maths applies to flatter goods: <a href=\"/all-weather-mats/\">all-weather floor mats</a> stack tighter than full trays, so a mixed container of both categories needs a plan rather than an estimate.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 6: documents for customs</h2>\n<p>Get the paperwork right once, and repeat it. The commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading and any origin documents should carry the same description, the same SKUs and the same quantities, with the carton count matching the packing list. Ask the factory for the HS code it uses for the shipment and check it against the code you normally declare at your own border, because a mismatch is resolved after the goods arrive, not before. Any market-specific laboratory reports or declarations you need should be requested as part of the order, with the scope of the report stated, since requirements differ by country.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 7: the shape of the timeline</h2>\n<p>No supplier can give a firm schedule before the project is defined, and one that does is guessing. What you can control is the sequence, and every stage waits for the one before it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fitment confirmed and tooling route chosen.</li>\n<li>Scan and pattern (only if a new mould is required).</li>\n<li>First-article sample and your approval.</li>\n<li>Bulk production and in-line checks.</li>\n<li>Packing, marking and container loading.</li>\n<li>Documents issued, and the booking you made against them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ask for a schedule with dates and named owners rather than a total, then watch which stage slips. Your own slowest step is usually artwork or sample approval, not the moulding, so the most effective thing you can do to shorten the project is to decide branding and packaging early.</p>\n\n<h2>Send the programme, not a part number</h2>\n<p>Give us the vehicle list with model years, the positions you want to cover, the drive side and the quantity per variant. <a href=\"/#finder\">Send the make, model and year</a> and we will tell you which moulds we already hold, which vehicles need a new one, and what the next sample step looks like.</p>"
 }
]

